🚨 Quality Gates per Perspective
Each perspective has minimum requirements before work proceeds:

🎯 Product Manager Quality Gate
 User need clearly articulated (not just "we want X")

 Success metric defined and measurable

 Priority justified relative to roadmap

🏗️ Architect Quality Gate
 At least 2 alternative approaches documented

 Scalability impact assessed (10M user scenario)

 Dependencies mapped (internal + external)

 Risks identified with mitigation plans

🎨 Designer Quality Gate
 User flow diagrammed (even for APIs: developer UX)

 Accessibility checklist completed (WCAG 2.1 AA minimum)

 Design mockup created (or rationale for skipping)

💻 Engineer Quality Gate
 Implementation plan step-by-step

 Code conventions reviewed

 Performance implications considered (Big-O, memory)

 Error handling strategy defined

🧪 QA Quality Gate
 Test plan written (unit, integration, E2E)

 At least 10 edge cases enumerated

 Acceptance criteria defined (specific, testable)

 Test coverage target set (>80%)

🔒 Security Quality Gate
 Threat model documented (STRIDE or similar)

 Sensitive data identified (PII, secrets, etc.)

 Auth/authz strategy confirmed

 Security checklist completed (OWASP Top 10)

🚀 DevOps Quality Gate
 Deployment plan documented (step-by-step)

 Monitoring/alerting defined (metrics, thresholds, actions)

 Rollback plan confirmed and tested

 Infrastructure requirements specified

No code merges to main without all relevant quality gates passing.

🌍 Language & Project Agnostic
This protocol works for:

Backend APIs (Python, Go, Node.js, Rust, Java...)

Mobile Apps (React Native, Flutter, Swift, Kotlin...)

Web Apps (React, Vue, Svelte, vanilla JS...)

CLI Tools (Bash, Python, Go...)

Data Pipelines (SQL, Airflow, Spark...)

Infrastructure (Terraform, Kubernetes, CloudFormation...)

ML/AI (TensorFlow, PyTorch, scikit-learn...)

The perspectives remain constant—only the emphasis shifts based on project type.

